<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cambyses II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 530–522 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cambyses II was the second king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, the son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a>, who ruled in the late sixth century BCE. He conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> after defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-iii">Pharaoh Psamtik III</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pelusium">Battle of Pelusium</a> in 525 BCE. Egypt was incorporated into the empire as its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twenty-seventh-dynasty-of-egypt">27th Dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii.jpg" alt="Cambyses II" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jona Lendering · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stela_Cambyses_Apis_closeup.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Cambyses II</b> extended these conquests by defeating Pharaoh Psamtik III at the Battle of Pelusium in 525 BCE, incorporating Egypt as its 27th Dynasty and extending Achaemenid authority across three continents.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His court attracted the poets Anacreon and Ibycus and the physician Democedes of Croton, and he made alliances first with Amasis of Egypt and later with <b>Cambyses II of Persia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/samos">Samos · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Such service had its reward: for supporting <b>Cambyses II</b>'s conquest of Egypt the Persian king granted Sidon Dor, Joppa and the Plain of Sharon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>He also says that when <b>Cambyses II</b> ordered an attack on Carthage, the Phoenicians refused to make war on their own children, and the plan was dropped.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Many non-Persians served the king too: the Egyptian priest and physician Udjahorresnet advised <b>Cambyses II</b> and Darius, and the Jewish courtier Nehemiah, by his own account, was sent to govern Judah.</blockquote><a href="/societies/achaemenid-persia">Social Hierarchy of the Achaemenid Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>In 525 BCE <b>Cambyses II</b> defeated Psamtik III of the Twenty-sixth Dynasty of Egypt at Pelusium and took Memphis, making Egypt a province of the Achaemenid Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/achaemenid-egypt">Achaemenid Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecbatana">Ecbatana</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecbatana">Ecbatana</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.81,48.52&amp;year=-526" aria-label="Ecbatana on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5781 904 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Cambyses II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6231 1174h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6231 1174h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.81,48.52&amp;year=-526">What was here in 526 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Ah! truly, do what they may, it is impossible for men to turn aside the coming fate.&#8221;</p><footer>To the Persian nobles, dying, 522 BCE (Herodotus, Histories, book 3) &#183; tr. George Rawlinson, 1858 &#183; Herodotus's account; he had killed his brother on the strength of a dream &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_History_of_Herodotus_(Rawlinson)/Book_3" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Do this, and then may your land bring you forth fruit abundantly, and your wives bear children, and your herds increase, and freedom be your portion for ever…&#8221;</p><footer>To the Persian nobles, dying, urging them to win back the throne from the Magi (Herodotus, Histories, book 3) &#183; tr. George Rawlinson, 1858 &#183; Herodotus's account &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_History_of_Herodotus_(Rawlinson)/Book_3" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
